  "lists": [],
  "hints": [
    "how is this different from plain Fibonacci - how many previous terms does each new term depend on?",
    "what if you built the sequence forward from T(0), T(1), T(2) instead of recursing down from n?",
    "you only ever need the last three computed values to get the next one",
    "iterate from 0 up to n, keeping three rolling variables and shifting them each step"
  ],
  "commonMistakes": [
    "reusing the two-variable Fibonacci pattern without accounting for the third term",
    "mixing up which of the three base cases (T(0), T(1), T(2)) apply for small n",
    "off-by-one errors when shifting the three rolling variables forward each iteration"
  ],
  "content": {
    "problemStatement": "A tribonacci sequence starts with T(0) = 0, T(1) = 1, T(2) = 1, and every term after that is the sum of the three terms before it: T(n) = T(n-1) + T(n-2) + T(n-3).\n\n**Given `n`, return T(n).**\n",
    "constraints": [
      "0 <= n <= 37",
      "T(0) = 0, T(1) = 1, T(2) = 1, and T(n) = T(n-1) + T(n-2) + T(n-3) for n >= 3"
    ],
    "notes": [
      "n = 0 (returns 0)",
      "n = 1 or n = 2 (both return 1)",
      "larger n where naive recursion becomes very slow"
    ],
    "inputFormat": "Arguments passed into solution function",
    "outputFormat": "Return output matching method signature",
